window.CalcPast["2021-2"] = {"year":"2021–2022","sitting":2,"kind":"Resit","date":"July 5, 2022","iso":"2022-07-05","minutes":120,"file":"calc-exam-2021-02","instructions":["You get 10 points for writing your name and student number on each page (the grade is (points + 10)/10).","For each item the maximum score is indicated; in total you can score 100 points.","A regular calculator is allowed; graphical/programmable calculators, phones and other devices are not.","You have 120 minutes (most students with extra time: 150 minutes).","Always explain your calculations. Do not write numbers in digits (e.g. 0.25) but use fractions (e.g. 1/4) and simplify as much as possible."],"questions":[{"no":"1","points":9,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"If $$\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{A\\cdot x^x+B\\ln(x)+Cx^3-2}{(x-1)^2}=-6$$ then what are the values of $A$, $B$, and $C$? [Hint: $A,B,C\\in\\mathbb{Z}$]","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$A =$","answer":"5","latex":"5"},{"kind":"number","label":"$B =$","answer":"4","latex":"4"},{"kind":"number","label":"$C =$","answer":"-3","latex":"-3"}],"points":9,"topic":"lhopital","hints":["The numerator must tend to $0$ (one equation); after one L'Hôpital step it must tend to $0$ again (second equation); the third step gives the value. Recall $(x^x)^{\\prime} = x^x(\\ln x + 1)$.","$A =$ The answer is a positive integer. $B =$ The answer is a positive integer. $C =$ The answer is a negative integer."],"solution":["Numerator $\\to 0$: $A + C - 2 = 0$.","L'Hôpital: $\\dfrac{Ax^x(\\ln x + 1) + \\frac Bx + 3Cx^2}{2(x-1)}$; numerator at $1$: $A + B + 3C = 0$.","L'Hôpital again: $\\dfrac{Ax^x((\\ln x+1)^2 + \\frac1x) - \\frac{B}{x^2} + 6Cx}{2} \\to \\dfrac{2A - B + 6C}{2} = -6$.","Solving: $A = 5$, $B = 4$, $C = -3$."]}]},{"no":"2","points":10,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Given is the following integral $$I=\\int\\frac{2x^5+4x^4+10x^3+22x^2+4x+5}{(x+1)^2(x^2+4)}\\,dx = \\int p(x)\\,dx+\\int\\frac{A}{x+1}\\,dx+\\int\\frac{B}{(x+1)^2}\\,dx+\\int\\frac{Cx+D}{x^2+4}\\,dx$$ Give $p(x)$ and $A,B,C,D\\in\\mathbb{Z}$. You don’t need to compute the integrals of each term.","parts":[{"kind":"expr","label":"$p(x) =$","answer":"2*x","latex":"2 x","vars":["x"],"sample":{"x":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"number","label":"$A =$","answer":"-2","latex":"-2"},{"kind":"number","label":"$B =$","answer":"3","latex":"3"},{"kind":"number","label":"$C =$","answer":"2","latex":"2"},{"kind":"number","label":"$D =$","answer":"1","latex":"1"}],"points":10,"topic":"partial-fractions","hints":["Expand the denominator ($x^4 + 2x^3 + 5x^2 + 8x + 4$) and do polynomial long division first; then decompose the remainder fraction.","$p(x) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 1 in $x$. $A =$ The answer is a negative integer. $B =$ The answer is a positive integer. $C =$ The answer is a positive integer. $D =$ The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$(x+1)^2(x^2+4) = x^4+2x^3+5x^2+8x+4$; long division gives quotient $2x$ and remainder $6x^2 - 4x + 5$.","$6x^2-4x+5 = A(x+1)(x^2+4) + B(x^2+4) + (Cx+D)(x+1)^2$.","Comparing coefficients: $A + C = 0$, $A + B + 2C + D = 6$, $4A + C + 2D = -4$, $4A + 4B + D = 5$.","Solution: $p(x) = 2x$, $A = -2$, $B = 3$, $C = 2$, $D = 1$."]}]},{"no":"3","points":15,"intro":"Select the best fitting answer.","items":[{"label":"(a)","prompt":"Match the graphs shown in Figure 1 to the following functions: i. $f(x)=xe^{-x}$; ii. $f(x)=e^x$; iii. $f(x)=e^{x^2}$; iv. $f(x)=e^{-x}$. [[img:assets/past-exams/2021-07-q3a-graphs.png|Four graphs: function A top left, B top right, C bottom left, D bottom right]] *Figure 1: Top left: Function A, Top right: Function B, Bottom left: Function C, Bottom right: Function D.*","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"i. $f(x) = xe^{-x}$","options":["Function A","Function B","Function C","Function D"],"answer":0,"latex":"Function A","points":1.5},{"kind":"choice","label":"ii. $f(x) = e^x$","options":["Function A","Function B","Function C","Function D"],"answer":2,"latex":"Function C","points":1.5},{"kind":"choice","label":"iii. $f(x) = e^{x^2}$","options":["Function A","Function B","Function C","Function D"],"answer":3,"latex":"Function D","points":1.5},{"kind":"choice","label":"iv. $f(x) = e^{-x}$","options":["Function A","Function B","Function C","Function D"],"answer":1,"latex":"Function B","points":1.5}],"points":6,"topic":"domain-range","hints":["Look at the behaviour for $x\\to\\pm\\infty$ and whether the graph is symmetric or has a maximum."],"solution":["$xe^{-x}$ goes to $-\\infty$ for $x\\to-\\infty$ and has a maximum at $x = 1$: A.","$e^x$ increases from $0$: C. $e^{x^2}$ is a symmetric U-shape: D. $e^{-x}$ decreases to $0$: B."]},{"label":"(b)","prompt":"Which of the following functions is not even?","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["$f(x)=0$;","$f(x)=\\cos(x)$;","$f(x)=x^4+x^2$;","None of the above."],"answer":3,"latex":"None of the above."}],"points":3,"topic":"even-odd","hints":["Check $f(-x) = f(x)$ for each one."],"solution":["All three satisfy $f(-x) = f(x)$ (the zero function is both even and odd). **(D)**"]},{"label":"(c)","prompt":"Let $f(x)$ be an odd function, and $g(x)$ be an even function. If $f, g$ are differentiable everywhere, then","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["$f'(x)$ is even, and $g'(x)$ is even.","$f'(x)$ is odd, and $g'(x)$ is even.","$f'(x)$ is even, and $g'(x)$ is odd.","$f'(x)$ is odd, and $g'(x)$ is odd."],"answer":2,"latex":"$f'(x)$ is even, and $g'(x)$ is odd."}],"points":3,"topic":"even-odd","hints":["Differentiate $f(-x) = -f(x)$ and $g(-x) = g(x)$ with the chain rule."],"solution":["$-f'(-x) = -f'(x) \\Rightarrow f'(-x) = f'(x)$: even.","$-g'(-x) = g'(x)$: odd. **(C)**"]},{"label":"(d)","prompt":"Which of the following functions is *not differentiable* in the most points on the interval $[-100,100]$? (A) $f(x)=\\begin{cases}x+2 & \\text{if } x<-1;\\\\ |x| & \\text{if } -1\\le x\\le1;\\\\ -x+2 & \\text{if } x>1.\\end{cases}$ (B) $f(x)=\\begin{cases}0 & \\text{if } x\\in\\mathbb{Q};\\\\ 1 & \\text{if } x\\in\\mathbb{R}.\\end{cases}$ (C) $f(x)=|\\sin(x)|$; (D) $f(x)=\\ln|x|+x^4-17$.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["(A)","(B)","(C)","(D)"],"answer":1,"latex":"(B)"}],"points":3,"topic":"continuity","hints":["Count the points where each function has a corner, a jump or is undefined. A function that jumps between $0$ and $1$ on every interval is nowhere continuous."],"solution":["(A) has corners at $x = -1, 0, 1$: 3 points. (C) has corners at the multiples of $\\pi$: 63 points. (D) fails only at $x = 0$.","(B) is the indicator-type function that is discontinuous everywhere, hence differentiable nowhere. **(B)**"]}]},{"no":"4","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Let $f_c(x)=\\frac14x^3$ for $0<x<c$ and $f_c(x)=0$ otherwise. For what value $c$ can $f_c(x)$ be a pdf?","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$c =$","answer":"2","latex":"2"}],"points":5,"topic":"continuous","hints":["$f_c \\ge 0$ for $c > 0$; set $\\int_0^c\\frac14x^3dx = 1$.","The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$\\int_0^c\\frac14x^3dx = \\frac1{16}c^4 = 1 \\iff c^4 = 16$.","$c > 0$, so $c = 2$."]}]},{"no":"5","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Compute the following integral using a trigonometric substitution $$\\int\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{9-x^2}}=A\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{x}{B}\\right)+C.$$ with $A,B\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and $C$ an arbitrary constant.","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$A =$","answer":"1","latex":"1"},{"kind":"number","label":"$B =$","answer":"3","latex":"3"}],"points":5,"topic":"substitution","hints":["Substitute $x = 3\\sin\\varphi$, so $dx = 3\\cos\\varphi\\,d\\varphi$ and $\\sqrt{9 - x^2} = 3\\cos\\varphi$.","$A =$ The answer is a positive integer. $B =$ The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$x = 3\\sin\\varphi$: $\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{3\\cos\\varphi\\,d\\varphi}{3\\cos\\varphi} = \\int d\\varphi = \\varphi + C = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac x3\\right) + C$.","$A = 1$, $B = 3$."]}]},{"no":"6","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"In the previous exam we saw that the expectation of a 41-sided dice with pips $1,2,\\dots,41$ has an expectation of 21. Let us now consider a doubling cube with pips $2,4,8,16,\\dots,2^n$. What is the minimal number of sides this doubling cube must have to also have an expectation of 21?","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$n =$","answer":"6","latex":"6"}],"points":5,"topic":"discrete","hints":["$E(X) = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n 2^i$. Try $n = 1, 2, 3, \\dots$","The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$E = \\frac1n(2 + 4 + \\dots + 2^n)$: $n=1$: $2$; $n=2$: $3$; $n=3$: $\\frac{14}3$; …","$n = 6$: $\\frac16(2+4+8+16+32+64) = 21$. So $6$ sides."]}]},{"no":"7","points":10,"intro":"Answer T(rue) or F(alse).","items":[{"label":"(a)","prompt":"Let $b$ be a positive root of $b^5-5b^2+1=0$, with $b\\approx1.66798$. The function $f(x)=x^4-2x$ for $-1<x<b$, and $f(x)=0$ otherwise, is a pdf.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"latex":"False"}],"points":2,"topic":"continuous","hints":["A pdf must be non-negative everywhere. Try a value such as $x = 1$."],"solution":["$f(1) = 1 - 2 = -1 < 0$, so it is not a pdf. **False**"]},{"label":"(b)","prompt":"Let $f(x)=|\\cos(x)|$. Its limit at $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ exists and is equal to $0$.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"latex":"True"}],"points":2,"topic":"limits","hints":["Limits are about values, not about derivatives."],"solution":["$|\\cos x|$ is continuous, so the limit is $|\\cos\\frac\\pi2| = 0$ (only the derivative fails to exist). **True**"]},{"label":"(c)","prompt":"For an even function $f(x)$, we have $\\int_0^af(x)dx=\\int_{-a}^0f(x)dx$.","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"latex":"True"}],"points":2,"topic":"even-odd","hints":["An even function is symmetric in the $y$-axis."],"solution":["Substitute $x\\mapsto -x$: the two areas are mirror images. **True**"]},{"label":"(d)","prompt":"$\\int\\frac{3x^2}{x^3}dx=3\\ln(x)+C$","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"latex":"False"}],"points":2,"topic":"substitution","hints":["What is the antiderivative of $\\frac1x$ on the whole domain $x \\neq 0$?"],"solution":["$\\int\\frac3x dx = 3\\ln|x| + C$ (absolute value needed). **False**"]},{"label":"(e)","prompt":"There exist polynomial functions $f(x):\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ that have zero roots. (A polynomial function is a function $f(x)=\\sum_{i=0}^na_ix^i$ for some $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and some $a_i\\in\\mathbb{R}$.)","parts":[{"kind":"choice","label":"","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"latex":"True"}],"points":2,"topic":"domain-range","hints":["Think of a parabola that stays above the $x$-axis."],"solution":["For example $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ has no real roots. **True**"]}]},{"no":"8","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Let $f(x,y)=\\frac12(e^y-e^{-y})\\sin(x)$. First, determine $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(x,y)$ and then use this result to determine the value $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(\\pi,\\ln(2))\\in\\mathbb{Q}$.","parts":[{"kind":"expr","label":"$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(x,y) =$","answer":"(exp(y) + exp(-y))*cos(x)/2","latex":"\\frac{\\left(e^{y} + e^{- y}\\right) \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{2}","vars":["x","y"],"sample":{"x":[-2.0,2.0],"y":[-1.0,1.0]}},{"kind":"number","label":"Value at $(\\pi,\\ln 2)$:","answer":"-5/4","latex":"- \\frac{5}{4}"}],"points":5,"topic":"partial-derivatives","hints":["Differentiate with respect to $y$ first ($\\frac{d}{dy}e^{-y} = -e^{-y}$), then with respect to $x$.","$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f(x,y) =$ The answer contains $e$ and a trigonometric function. Value at $(\\pi,\\ln 2)$: The answer is a negative fraction (not an integer)."],"solution":["$\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}f = \\frac12(e^y + e^{-y})\\sin x$; $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}$: $\\frac12(e^y+e^{-y})\\cos x$.","At $(\\pi,\\ln2)$: $\\frac12(2 + \\frac12)(-1) = -\\frac54$."]}]},{"no":"9","points":5,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Find a point $(x,y)$ on $f(x)=x^3-6x+7$ where the tangent line to $f$ in $(x,y)$ is parallel to the tangent line to $f$ at $(-3,2)$.","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"$x =$","answer":"3","latex":"3"},{"kind":"number","label":"$y =$","answer":"16","latex":"16"}],"points":5,"topic":"implicit-tangent","hints":["Parallel lines have equal slopes: solve $f^{\\prime}(x) = f^{\\prime}(-3)$ for the other solution.","$x =$ The answer is a positive integer. $y =$ The answer is a positive integer."],"solution":["$f'(x) = 3x^2 - 6$, $f'(-3) = 21$.","$3x^2 - 6 = 21 \\iff x = \\pm3$; the other point is $x = 3$, $y = f(3) = 16$."]}]},{"no":"10","points":10,"intro":"","items":[{"label":"","prompt":"Compute the following integral using partial integration $$\\int_0^\\pi\\frac1\\pi x^2\\cos(4x)\\,dx=\\frac1\\pi\\left(p(x)\\sin(4x)\\Big|_0^\\pi-\\int_0^\\pi q(x)\\sin(4x)\\,dx\\right)=\\frac1\\pi\\Big(\\big(r(x)\\sin(4x)+s(x)\\cos(4x)\\big)\\Big|_0^\\pi\\Big)=A$$ with $p(x),q(x),r(x)$, and $s(x)$ polynomials in $x$ and $A\\in\\mathbb{Q}$.","parts":[{"kind":"expr","label":"$p(x) =$","answer":"x^2/4","latex":"\\frac{x^{2}}{4}","vars":["x"],"sample":{"x":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"expr","label":"$q(x) =$","answer":"x/2","latex":"\\frac{x}{2}","vars":["x"],"sample":{"x":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"expr","label":"$r(x) =$","answer":"x^2/4 - 1/32","latex":"\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{1}{32}","vars":["x"],"sample":{"x":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"expr","label":"$s(x) =$","answer":"x/8","latex":"\\frac{x}{8}","vars":["x"],"sample":{"x":[-2.0,2.0]}},{"kind":"number","label":"$A =$","answer":"1/8","latex":"\\frac{1}{8}"}],"points":10,"topic":"by-parts","hints":["By parts twice: first $f = x^2$, $g^{\\prime} = \\cos(4x)$ (so $g = \\frac14\\sin 4x$), then $f = x$, $g^{\\prime} = \\sin(4x)$.","$p(x) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 2 in $x$. $q(x) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 1 in $x$. $r(x) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 2 in $x$. $s(x) =$ The answer is a polynomial of degree 1 in $x$. $A =$ The answer is a positive fraction (not an integer)."],"solution":["First step: $p(x) = \\frac14x^2$ and $q(x) = \\frac12x$.","$\\int\\frac12x\\sin(4x)dx = -\\frac18x\\cos(4x) + \\frac1{32}\\sin(4x)$.","So the antiderivative is $\\big(\\frac14x^2 - \\frac1{32}\\big)\\sin(4x) + \\frac18x\\cos(4x)$: $r(x) = \\frac14x^2 - \\frac1{32}$, $s(x) = \\frac18x$.","$A = \\frac1\\pi\\big(\\frac18\\pi\\cos 4\\pi - 0\\big) = \\frac18$."]}]},{"no":"11","points":11,"intro":"Compute the following limits:","items":[{"label":"(a)","prompt":"$\\lim_{x\\to1}\\dfrac{x^3-7x+6}{x^2-1}$","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"Limit:","answer":"-2","latex":"-2"}],"points":5,"topic":"limits","hints":["Factor: $x = 1$ is a root of both numerator and denominator.","The answer is a negative integer."],"solution":["$\\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-1)(x+1)} = \\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{x+1} \\to \\frac{-4}{2} = -2$."]},{"label":"(b)","prompt":"$\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}(e^t+t)^{\\frac1t}$","parts":[{"kind":"number","label":"Limit:","answer":"e","latex":"e"}],"points":6,"topic":"lhopital","hints":["Form $\\infty^0$: take the logarithm, $\\ln L = \\lim\\frac{\\ln(e^t + t)}{t}$, and apply L'Hôpital.","The exact answer is positive and involves $e$."],"solution":["$\\ln L = \\lim\\frac{\\ln(e^t+t)}{t} \\overset{LH}{=} \\lim\\frac{e^t + 1}{e^t + t} \\overset{LH}{=} \\lim\\frac{e^t}{e^t + 1} \\overset{LH}{=} 1$.","So $L = e$."]}]}],"id":"2021-2"};
